  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Widespread rain Thursday night through Friday. \n\nMainly dry weather will prevail tonight but we will see NW upslope \nclouds across the region. \n\nDry weather to continue Thursday ahead of a weak low tracking \ntowards the region which will bring a soaking rainfall to the region \nThursday night. \n\nA period of widespread rain is expected Thursday night through \nFriday. A southern stream trough ejecting into the central Plains \nThursday morning will phase with a strong northern stream trough \nover the Upper Midwest Thursday night, with associated strong \nmid/upper level forcing supporting a deepening surface low passing \nover the eastern Great Lakes Thursday night through early Friday. \nStrong forcing and moisture transport with this system will support \nrain overspreading the region from southwest to northeast Thursday \nnight, with periods of rain continuing Friday through Friday \nevening. \n\nRainfall amounts of 0.50\" to 1.0\" are likely with this system. It \nhas been quite dry across much of the region over the past month, so \nthis will be beneficial rain. There may be just enough instability\n(a few hundred J/kg of elevated instability) to support a few \nembedded thunderstorms as well, especially if the surface low \ntracks just north of the region.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Much colder Friday night through Saturday night with \na few wet snowflakes possible.\n\nThe surface low will continue to deepen as it moves down the Saint \nLawrence Valley Friday night, and then into the Canadian Maritimes \nSaturday. Strong cold advection within the sharp mid level trough \nwill deliver the coldest airmass of the fall season thus far by late \nFriday night and Saturday. DPVA/height falls from the mid level \ntrough and increasing lake induced instability will support the \ncontinuation of showers Friday night. \n\nThe boundary layer may become cold enough to support a few wet \nsnowflakes by late Friday night and Saturday morning. Despite strong \nlake induced instability, short fetch northwest flow and a rapidly \ndrying airmass will keep the lake response muted and brief. In terms \nof the potential for the first wet snowflakes of fall, it will be a \nrace between the drying airmass and arrival of sufficiently cold \nair. There is a low chance of a very minor coating of slush on the \ngrass across high terrain Saturday morning, otherwise no \naccumulation and zero impact is expected. Any rain/snow showers will \nend by midday Saturday with ongoing drying and subsidence. \n\nTemperatures this period will be much cooler, with lows dropping \ninto the 30s Friday night and only recovering to the 40s for highs \nSaturday. It will be blustery as well, with wind gusts in the 25-35 \nmph range Friday night through Saturday, and up to 45 mph along the \nlakeshores. Low temperatures will drop into the 30s again Saturday \nnight, although winds may never become light enough to allow for \nfrost formation. \n\nThe cooldown will be very brief, with above average temperatures \nquickly returning early next week.",
